<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Act of Settlement 1662</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1662">1662</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-settlement-1701">Act of Settlement</a> 1662 was a law of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-ireland">Irish Parliament</a> that set out the terms for redistributing land in Ireland after the Restoration of Charles II. It largely confirmed Cromwellian soldiers and adventurers in the lands confiscated from Catholics after the rising of 1641, while allowing some Catholics judged 'innocent' to recover estates. The Act of Explanation of 1665 modified it, and Catholic landholding remained greatly reduced.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the Cromwellian conquest of Ireland, the Rump Parliament in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> passed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-settlement-1701">Act of Settlement</a> 1652. It provided for the summary execution of leaders of the Irish Rebellion of 1641 and for confiscating enough land to repay the City of London's loans under the Adventurers' Acts and to reward the soldiers, most of whom sold their claims on. Confiscated lands east of the Shannon were to be cleared, their owners moved to Connacht and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-clare">County Clare</a>. Protestant Royalists could avoid confiscation by paying fines, Catholics could not, and Catholic landholding fell from 60 per cent before the wars to 8 or 9 per cent under the Commonwealth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>At the Restoration, Royalists led by the Duke of Ormonde pressed the king to undo these injustices. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-ireland">Parliament of Ireland</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, still Protestant only, passed the new act on 30 May 1662. It ordered Cromwellian settlers to give up part of their land to the Old English and to innocent Catholics, meaning those who had been Royalists and had not taken part in massacres of Protestant settlers, as commissioners would decide. But the settlers were to be compensated with equal land elsewhere, and there was not enough land for this, so only the richer Catholic landowners recovered estates, among them Viscount Dillon and Donough MacCarty, 1st Earl of Clancarty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>A Court of Claims under Sir Richard Raynsford found too many Catholics innocent, and the Act of Explanation 1665 required Cromwellian settlers, with some named exceptions, to give up a third of the lands they had received. A favoured minority, mostly Old English Royalists, recovered most of their estates, while former Confederates got little and many felt betrayed by the Stuart monarchy; the poet Dáibhí Ó Bruadair called the Restoration Purgatory for Irish Catholics.</p><p>Many Protestants thought the settlement too lenient. Catholic ownership stood at 22 per cent by 1685. The historian Jane Ohlmeyer argued that rank mattered more than religion in these awards, which were not concluded until about 1670. In 1689 the Patriot Parliament of James II repealed the Cromwellian settlement, before the Williamite victory brought new confiscations.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Act of Settlement 1662” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
